.TH FETCH 1 "2026-05-22" "hako" "hako utilities"
.SH NAME
fetch \- make HTTP requests and print responses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fetch
.RB [ \-X
.IR method ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR body ]
.IR url \ [...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fetch
sends an HTTP request to each
.I url
and writes the response body to standard output.
If multiple URLs are given their responses are concatenated in order.
.PP
Supported methods are
.BR GET ,
.BR HEAD ,
.BR POST ,
.BR PUT ,
.BR PATCH ,
.BR DELETE ,
and
.BR OPTIONS .
The default method is
.BR GET .
Providing
.B \-d
without an explicit
.B \-X
switches the method to
.BR POST .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-X " method "\fR,\fP \-\-request " method
Use
.I method
as the HTTP request method.
.TP
.BI "\-d " body "\fR,\fP \-\-data " body
Send
.I body
as the request body.
Implicitly sets the method to
.B POST
unless
.B \-X
is also given.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B "fetch https://example.com"
Fetch a URL with GET.
.TP
.B "fetch -X DELETE https://api.example.com/resource/1"
Send a DELETE request.
.TP
.B "fetch -d 'name=hako' https://api.example.com/items"
POST form data.
.TP
.B "fetch -X PUT -d 'name=hako' https://api.example.com/items/1"
PUT with an explicit method.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requests succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Missing URL, unsupported method, or a network error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR wget (1),
.BR hako (1)
